{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_azqCrNDh8om92AUb5JCevDnL","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tlayout: layouts/base.vto\n3\ttitle: Why NetScript\n4\ttemplateEngine: [vento, md]\n5\t---\n6\t\n7\t{{ comp.hero({\n8\t  tagline: \"For teams whose TypeScript app has become a system.\",\n9\t  subhead: \"NetScript fits long-lived products where request handling, background work, browser UI, and operations have become one application; it trades pick-any-library freedom for a coordinated Deno workspace.\",\n10\t  ctas: [\n11\t    { label: \"Start a workspace\", href: \"/quickstart/\", primary: true },\n12\t    { label: \"Read the mental model\", href: \"/concepts/\" }\n13\t  ]\n14\t}) }}\n15\t\n16\tIf the product is a static site, a short-lived prototype, or one request/response process, start\n17\twith Fresh or Hono. NetScript begins to pay for itself when the boundaries between processes,\n18\tbackground work, and the browser have become part of the product.\n19\t\n20\t## The integration tax\n21\t\n22\tYou shouldn't have to assemble a backend from a dozen libraries that have never met. The cost is\n23\tnot choosing them once; it is maintaining the seams between them:\n24\t\n25\t1. **The service and browser disagree.** Request and response shapes drift until a production call\n26\t   finds the mismatch.\n27\t2. **A retry loop stands in for durable state.** Multi-step workflows lack recorded checkpoints or\n28\t   explicit rollbacks, leaving the system half-executed when a process dies.\n29\t3. **Resources and telemetry are assembled separately.** Queue, service, database, health, and\n30\t   trace configuration drift, so an incident crosses several tools before it becomes an\n31\t   explanation.\n32\t\n33\tThat standing maintenance is the **integration tax**: unrelated tools pretending to be one system.\n34\t\n35\t## The two decisions that are expensive to retrofit\n36\t\n37\tWe didn't invent new primitives. NetScript makes two early architecture choices and carries them\n38\tthrough the workspace: callers derive their types from shared contracts, and multi-step work runs\n39\tthrough explicit durable state. Logging can be added later. Replacing parallel client definitions\n40\tor ad-hoc effects after they spread through an application usually means changing every caller and\n41\tevery workflow.\n42\t\n43\tThe rest of NetScript makes those two decisions operational. Services serve the contract at request\n44\ttime, plugin runtimes own the work that outlives a request, and Aspire brings up the resources with\n45\ttheir telemetry connected. The implementation details live in [Core concepts](/concepts/).\n46\t\n47\t## Compare the center of gravity\n48\t\n49\tNo row is a universal winner. Choose the system whose primary constraint matches yours.\n50\t\n51\t{{ comp.apiTable({\n52\t  caption: \"Where each choice starts, and what NetScript changes\",\n53\t  rows: [\n54\t    {\n55\t      name: \"Fresh or Hono\",\n56\t      type: \"The web app or small API is the whole system\",\n57\t      desc: \"NetScript uses Fresh and Hono as foundations, then adds service contracts, background runtimes, and an Aspire workspace. The cost is more framework and more project shape.\"\n58\t    },\n59\t    {\n60\t      name: \"Next.js\",\n61\t      type: \"React and the Next ecosystem are the center\",\n62\t      desc: \"NetScript chooses Deno with Fresh and Preact, plus explicit service and durable-runtime boundaries. It is not the React/Next path; choose the web ecosystem your product needs.\"\n63\t    },\n64\t    {\n65\t      name: \"Encore-style stack\",\n66\t      type: \"Integrated infrastructure workflow is the center\",\n67\t      desc: \"NetScript keeps a Deno/JSR workspace in the application and puts Aspire behind an adapter. It provides less managed infrastructure, so deployment and operations remain your responsibility.\"\n68\t    },\n69\t    {\n70\t      name: \"Temporal\",\n71\t      type: \"A dedicated workflow platform is the main decision\",\n72\t      desc: \"NetScript's saga core keeps TypeScript definitions, store and bus ports, transports, and compensation inside a broader application workspace. It is not a drop-in Temporal API.\"\n73\t    },\n74\t    {\n75\t      name: \"Assemble it yourself\",\n76\t      type: \"Maximum component choice is a requirement\",\n77\t      desc: \"You can choose every queue, tracer, service layer, and runtime independently. NetScript constrains those choices; assembling them yourself leaves every integration seam with your team.\"\n78\t    }\n79\t  ]\n80\t}) }}\n81\t\n82\t## The trade-offs are real\n83\t\n84\t- **The package family is pre-1.0.** APIs move, so scaffolded imports use exact\n85\t  `{{ releaseSpecifier }}` pins. If you need a frozen surface today, wait or budget for upgrades.\n86\t- **The .NET Aspire CLI is the default orchestration path.** Scaffold with `--no-aspire` to opt out;\n87\t  resource wiring and local telemetry then belong to you. [See the Aspire boundary](/quickstart/aspire/).\n88\t- **The web layer is Fresh and Preact.** That is a deliberate fit, not compatibility with the\n89\t  React/Next ecosystem. [See the web layer](/web-layer/).\n90\t- **A coordinated workspace is broader than a one-process app needs.** A small service pays the\n91\t  structure cost before durable work, plugins, and multi-resource operations pay it back.\n92\t- **NetScript is not a hosted platform.** You own the workspace and the deployment decisions.\n93\t\n94\tIf those are the problems and trade-offs you recognize, [start the workspace](/quickstart/).\n95\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a83c9845-4a4d-49af-b9fe-4d6db86478d5","uuid":"b3b7426e-4686-4fc8-b48f-261adb9bcb1b","timestamp":"2026-08-04T10:25:06.595Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/codex/repos/ns-mainpages/docs/site/why.vto","content":"---\nlayout: layouts/base.vto\ntitle: Why NetScript\ntemplateEngine: [vento, md]\n---\n\n{{ comp.hero({\n  tagline: \"For teams whose TypeScript app has become a system.\",\n  subhead: \"NetScript fits long-lived products where request handling, background work, browser UI, and operations have become one application; it trades pick-any-library freedom for a coordinated Deno workspace.\",\n  ctas: [\n    { label: \"Start a workspace\", href: \"/quickstart/\", primary: true },\n    { label: \"Read the mental model\", href: \"/concepts/\" }\n  ]\n}) }}\n\nIf the product is a static site, a short-lived prototype, or one request/response process, start\nwith Fresh or Hono. NetScript begins to pay for itself when the boundaries between processes,\nbackground work, and the browser have become part of the product.\n\n## The integration tax\n\nYou shouldn't have to assemble a backend from a dozen libraries that have never met. The cost is\nnot choosing them once; it is maintaining the seams between them:\n\n1. **The service and browser disagree.** Request and response shapes drift until a production call\n   finds the mismatch.\n2. **A retry loop stands in for durable state.** Multi-step workflows lack recorded checkpoints or\n   explicit rollbacks, leaving the system half-executed when a process dies.\n3. **Resources and telemetry are assembled separately.** Queue, service, database, health, and\n   trace configuration drift, so an incident crosses several tools before it becomes an\n   explanation.\n\nThat standing maintenance is the **integration tax**: unrelated tools pretending to be one system.\n\n## The two decisions that are expensive to retrofit\n\nWe didn't invent new primitives. NetScript makes two early architecture choices and carries them\nthrough the workspace: callers derive their types from shared contracts, and multi-step work runs\nthrough explicit durable state. Logging can be added later. Replacing parallel client definitions\nor ad-hoc effects after they spread through an application usually means changing every caller and\nevery workflow.\n\nThe rest of NetScript makes those two decisions operational. Services serve the contract at request\ntime, plugin runtimes own the work that outlives a request, and Aspire brings up the resources with\ntheir telemetry connected. The implementation details live in [Core concepts](/concepts/).\n\n## Compare the center of gravity\n\nNo row is a universal winner. Choose the system whose primary constraint matches yours.\n\n{{ comp.apiTable({\n  caption: \"Where each choice starts, and what NetScript changes\",\n  rows: [\n    {\n      name: \"Fresh or Hono\",\n      type: \"The web app or small API is the whole system\",\n      desc: \"NetScript uses Fresh and Hono as foundations, then adds service contracts, background runtimes, and an Aspire workspace. The cost is more framework and more project shape.\"\n    },\n    {\n      name: \"Next.js\",\n      type: \"React and the Next ecosystem are the center\",\n      desc: \"NetScript chooses Deno with Fresh and Preact, plus explicit service and durable-runtime boundaries. It is not the React/Next path; choose the web ecosystem your product needs.\"\n    },\n    {\n      name: \"Encore-style stack\",\n      type: \"Integrated infrastructure workflow is the center\",\n      desc: \"NetScript keeps a Deno/JSR workspace in the application and puts Aspire behind an adapter. It provides less managed infrastructure, so deployment and operations remain your responsibility.\"\n    },\n    {\n      name: \"Temporal\",\n      type: \"A dedicated workflow platform is the main decision\",\n      desc: \"NetScript's saga core keeps TypeScript definitions, store and bus ports, transports, and compensation inside a broader application workspace. It is not a drop-in Temporal API.\"\n    },\n    {\n      name: \"Assemble it yourself\",\n      type: \"Maximum component choice is a requirement\",\n      desc: \"You can choose every queue, tracer, service layer, and runtime independently. NetScript constrains those choices; assembling them yourself leaves every integration seam with your team.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}) }}\n\n## The trade-offs are real\n\n- **The package family is pre-1.0.** APIs move, so scaffolded imports use exact\n  `{{ releaseSpecifier }}` pins. If you need a frozen surface today, wait or budget for upgrades.\n- **The .NET Aspire CLI is the default orchestration path.** Scaffold with `--no-aspire` to opt out;\n  resource wiring and local telemetry then belong to you. [See the Aspire boundary](/quickstart/aspire/).\n- **The web layer is Fresh and Preact.** That is a deliberate fit, not compatibility with the\n  React/Next ecosystem. [See the web layer](/web-layer/).\n- **A coordinated workspace is broader than a one-process app needs.** A small service pays the\n  structure cost before durable work, plugins, and multi-resource operations pay it back.\n- **NetScript is not a hosted platform.** You own the workspace and the deployment decisions.\n\nIf those are the problems and trade-offs you recognize, [start the workspace](/quickstart/).\n","numLines":95,"startLine":1,"totalLines":95}}}
