{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_BSc0NMuGlWrkRczSEyv48FnA","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tlayout: layouts/base.vto\n3\ttitle: Quickstart\n4\ttemplateEngine: [vento, md]\n5\t---\n6\t\n7\t# Run a NetScript workspace, then change it\n8\t\n9\tThis path installs the CLI, scaffolds one example service, starts the generated resource graph,\n10\tand makes one visible edit.\n11\t\n12\t{{ comp callout { type: \"note\", title: \"Prerequisites\" } }}\n13\tInstall <strong><a href=\"https://docs.deno.com\">Deno 2.x</a></strong>, the\n14\t<strong><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/aspire/\">.NET Aspire CLI</a></strong>, and start\n15\tDocker before continuing. The default Postgres and cache resources run in containers.\n16\t{{ /comp }}\n17\t\n18\t## 1. Install the CLI\n19\t\n20\t```bash\n21\tdeno install --global --allow-all --name netscript jsr:@netscript/cli{{ releaseSpecifier }}\n22\t```\n23\t\n24\t## 2. Scaffold the workspace\n25\t\n26\t```bash\n27\tnetscript init my-app --db postgres --service --yes\n28\t```\n29\t\n30\tThe `--service` flag is explicit: without it, `netscript init` does not scaffold the example oRPC\n31\tservice. `--yes` accepts the remaining defaults without prompts, including the `dashboard` app name\n32\tand shared Redis cache used below.\n33\t\n34\t## 3. Start the resource graph\n35\t\n36\t**Nothing runs until this step.**\n37\t\n38\t```bash\n39\tcd my-app/aspire\n40\taspire restore   # first run only\n41\taspire start\n42\t```\n43\t\n44\tUse the dashboard URL and one-time login token printed by `aspire start`. Wait until the Fresh app,\n45\texample service, Postgres, and shared cache report healthy, then open the Fresh app from its resource\n46\tlink. The page heading should read `my-app`.\n47\t\n48\t{{ comp callout { type: \"note\", title: \"Aspire comes before any database command\" } }}\n49\tThe AppHost must start Postgres before a migration, generation, or seed command can connect to it.\n50\tContinue with <a href=\"/data-persistence/how-to/database-migration/\">Database migrations</a> when\n51\tyou need the database workflow.\n52\t{{ /comp }}\n53\t\n54\t## 4. Make your first change\n55\t\n56\tOpen `apps/dashboard/routes/(_components)/home-view.tsx`. Find the wrapped introductory sentence\n57\tthat starts with `A generated NetScript workspace` and replace that sentence with\n58\t`This checkout dashboard is ours.` Save the file, then refresh the Fresh app URL from Aspire. The\n59\tnew sentence should appear below the `my-app` heading while the app remains healthy.\n60\t\n61\t[See how the web layer is structured](/web-layer/).\n62\t\n63\tWhen you are finished, stop the AppHost from the `aspire/` directory:\n64\t\n65\t```bash\n66\taspire stop --apphost ./apphost.mts\n67\t```\n68\t\n69\tContinue with [the Storefront tutorial](/tutorials/storefront/) or [the mental model](/concepts/).\n70\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a83c9845-4a4d-49af-b9fe-4d6db86478d5","uuid":"ffbcb5a1-0889-4422-96ef-ac383056c744","timestamp":"2026-08-04T10:25:06.902Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/codex/repos/ns-mainpages/docs/site/quickstart.vto","content":"---\nlayout: layouts/base.vto\ntitle: Quickstart\ntemplateEngine: [vento, md]\n---\n\n# Run a NetScript workspace, then change it\n\nThis path installs the CLI, scaffolds one example service, starts the generated resource graph,\nand makes one visible edit.\n\n{{ comp callout { type: \"note\", title: \"Prerequisites\" } }}\nInstall <strong><a href=\"https://docs.deno.com\">Deno 2.x</a></strong>, the\n<strong><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/aspire/\">.NET Aspire CLI</a></strong>, and start\nDocker before continuing. The default Postgres and cache resources run in containers.\n{{ /comp }}\n\n## 1. Install the CLI\n\n```bash\ndeno install --global --allow-all --name netscript jsr:@netscript/cli{{ releaseSpecifier }}\n```\n\n## 2. Scaffold the workspace\n\n```bash\nnetscript init my-app --db postgres --service --yes\n```\n\nThe `--service` flag is explicit: without it, `netscript init` does not scaffold the example oRPC\nservice. `--yes` accepts the remaining defaults without prompts, including the `dashboard` app name\nand shared Redis cache used below.\n\n## 3. Start the resource graph\n\n**Nothing runs until this step.**\n\n```bash\ncd my-app/aspire\naspire restore   # first run only\naspire start\n```\n\nUse the dashboard URL and one-time login token printed by `aspire start`. Wait until the Fresh app,\nexample service, Postgres, and shared cache report healthy, then open the Fresh app from its resource\nlink. The page heading should read `my-app`.\n\n{{ comp callout { type: \"note\", title: \"Aspire comes before any database command\" } }}\nThe AppHost must start Postgres before a migration, generation, or seed command can connect to it.\nContinue with <a href=\"/data-persistence/how-to/database-migration/\">Database migrations</a> when\nyou need the database workflow.\n{{ /comp }}\n\n## 4. Make your first change\n\nOpen `apps/dashboard/routes/(_components)/home-view.tsx`. Find the wrapped introductory sentence\nthat starts with `A generated NetScript workspace` and replace that sentence with\n`This checkout dashboard is ours.` Save the file, then refresh the Fresh app URL from Aspire. The\nnew sentence should appear below the `my-app` heading while the app remains healthy.\n\n[See how the web layer is structured](/web-layer/).\n\nWhen you are finished, stop the AppHost from the `aspire/` directory:\n\n```bash\naspire stop --apphost ./apphost.mts\n```\n\nContinue with [the Storefront tutorial](/tutorials/storefront/) or [the mental model](/concepts/).\n","numLines":70,"startLine":1,"totalLines":70}}}
