{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_TAol6zXocVBw2cOk6TXlfX2F","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tlayout: layouts/base.vto\n3\ttitle: Core concepts\n4\ttemplateEngine: [vento, md]\n5\t---\n6\t\n7\t{{ comp.hero({\n8\t  tagline: \"A NetScript app is five connected layers.\",\n9\t  subhead: \"Contracts define the boundaries. Everything else — services, plugin runtimes, the web layer, the observed resource graph — carries them somewhere.\",\n10\t  ctas: [\n11\t    { label: \"Start a workspace\", href: \"/quickstart/\", primary: true },\n12\t    { label: \"Build the Storefront\", href: \"/tutorials/storefront/\" }\n13\t  ]\n14\t}) }}\n15\t\n16\t{{ comp.diagram({ src: \"/assets/diagrams/architecture-overview.svg\", alt: \"A NetScript workspace: contracts define services; services and plugin runtimes connect to the Fresh web app; the AppHost materializes the resources and their infrastructure under one observed graph.\", caption: \"The mental model: contracts → services → plugins → web layer, with Aspire and telemetry connecting the running resources.\" }) }}\n17\t\n18\t## 1. Contracts define the boundary\n19\t\n20\tThe contract is the source of truth for an operation's input and output. A service implements that\n21\toRPC contract, while `@netscript/sdk` derives clients and query helpers from the same contract map.\n22\tThe service runtime also uses its contract router for OpenAPI, so the TypeScript caller and the HTTP\n23\tdescription do not need parallel hand-written shapes.\n24\t\n25\tContracts describe behavior, not just entity CRUD: `checkout.start` is as natural a boundary as a\n26\tlist or update operation. Read [contracts and type flow](/explanation/contracts/) for the schemas\n27\tand router mechanics.\n28\t\n29\t## 2. Services execute request-time behavior\n30\t\n31\t`defineService()` turns a router into a running Hono and oRPC service with request logging, OpenAPI,\n32\tScalar docs, RPC, service information, and health endpoints configured by the preset. SDK clients\n33\tresolve service URLs lazily from Aspire-injected environment values, so callers do not pin a\n34\tlocal port into application code.\n35\t\n36\tA service owns the request boundary. Work that must resume after the process exits belongs in a\n37\tdurable runtime, not in a longer request handler. Go deeper in [Services & SDK](/services-sdk/) and\n38\t[service discovery](/services-sdk/how-to/discover-services/).\n39\t\n40\t## 3. Plugins add runtime capabilities\n41\t\n42\tThe host is empty; plugins fill it. A plugin manifest is validated data that declares services,\n43\tbackground processors, schemas, topics, configuration, and telemetry contributions. The CLI turns\n44\tthose declarations into static workspace wiring, and Aspire can materialize a plugin's API and\n45\tbackground processors as separate resources.\n46\t\n47\t“One plugin” is a packaging unit, not a runtime unit. Workers, sagas, triggers, and streams are the\n48\tdurable examples; the full manifest and registry mechanics live in\n49\t[the plugin-system explanation](/explanation/plugin-system/).\n50\t\n51\t## 4. The web layer carries the boundary to the user\n52\t\n53\t`@netscript/fresh` builds on Fresh 2 and Preact. Typed route contracts and `definePage()` connect\n54\tpaths, search parameters, metadata, handlers, data layers, and forms. A server page can call the\n55\tcontract-derived SDK, hydrate TanStack Query state, and hand interactive work to islands; routes\n56\tthat need no island ship no island JavaScript.\n57\t\n58\tUse [the web-layer overview](/web-layer/) for the complete model, then follow the focused guides for\n59\t[server rendering](/web-layer/server/), [page builders](/web-layer/builders/), and\n60\t[queries and islands](/web-layer/query/).\n61\t\n62\t## 5. Observability connects the resource graph\n63\t\n64\tOne runtime, many resources. The generated AppHost composes apps, services, plugin processors,\n65\tdatabases, and cache resources, then injects service-discovery and OTLP configuration into their\n66\tprocesses. The Aspire dashboard reads health, logs, traces, and metrics from that running graph;\n67\ttrace context carried by SDK calls keeps client and service spans connected.\n68\t\n69\tAspire is the default local orchestration layer, not part of every package API. Without it, the Deno\n70\tpackages remain usable, but resource startup, discovery values, and telemetry wiring become the\n71\tapplication's responsibility. Read [Orchestration & runtime](/orchestration-runtime/),\n72\t[Observability](/observability/), or [the Aspire boundary](/explanation/aspire/) for mechanics.\n73\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a83c9845-4a4d-49af-b9fe-4d6db86478d5","uuid":"edebf8e5-1a42-4f65-941e-ef73b23ba009","timestamp":"2026-08-04T10:25:07.000Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/codex/repos/ns-mainpages/docs/site/concepts.vto","content":"---\nlayout: layouts/base.vto\ntitle: Core concepts\ntemplateEngine: [vento, md]\n---\n\n{{ comp.hero({\n  tagline: \"A NetScript app is five connected layers.\",\n  subhead: \"Contracts define the boundaries. Everything else — services, plugin runtimes, the web layer, the observed resource graph — carries them somewhere.\",\n  ctas: [\n    { label: \"Start a workspace\", href: \"/quickstart/\", primary: true },\n    { label: \"Build the Storefront\", href: \"/tutorials/storefront/\" }\n  ]\n}) }}\n\n{{ comp.diagram({ src: \"/assets/diagrams/architecture-overview.svg\", alt: \"A NetScript workspace: contracts define services; services and plugin runtimes connect to the Fresh web app; the AppHost materializes the resources and their infrastructure under one observed graph.\", caption: \"The mental model: contracts → services → plugins → web layer, with Aspire and telemetry connecting the running resources.\" }) }}\n\n## 1. Contracts define the boundary\n\nThe contract is the source of truth for an operation's input and output. A service implements that\noRPC contract, while `@netscript/sdk` derives clients and query helpers from the same contract map.\nThe service runtime also uses its contract router for OpenAPI, so the TypeScript caller and the HTTP\ndescription do not need parallel hand-written shapes.\n\nContracts describe behavior, not just entity CRUD: `checkout.start` is as natural a boundary as a\nlist or update operation. Read [contracts and type flow](/explanation/contracts/) for the schemas\nand router mechanics.\n\n## 2. Services execute request-time behavior\n\n`defineService()` turns a router into a running Hono and oRPC service with request logging, OpenAPI,\nScalar docs, RPC, service information, and health endpoints configured by the preset. SDK clients\nresolve service URLs lazily from Aspire-injected environment values, so callers do not pin a\nlocal port into application code.\n\nA service owns the request boundary. Work that must resume after the process exits belongs in a\ndurable runtime, not in a longer request handler. Go deeper in [Services & SDK](/services-sdk/) and\n[service discovery](/services-sdk/how-to/discover-services/).\n\n## 3. Plugins add runtime capabilities\n\nThe host is empty; plugins fill it. A plugin manifest is validated data that declares services,\nbackground processors, schemas, topics, configuration, and telemetry contributions. The CLI turns\nthose declarations into static workspace wiring, and Aspire can materialize a plugin's API and\nbackground processors as separate resources.\n\n“One plugin” is a packaging unit, not a runtime unit. Workers, sagas, triggers, and streams are the\ndurable examples; the full manifest and registry mechanics live in\n[the plugin-system explanation](/explanation/plugin-system/).\n\n## 4. The web layer carries the boundary to the user\n\n`@netscript/fresh` builds on Fresh 2 and Preact. Typed route contracts and `definePage()` connect\npaths, search parameters, metadata, handlers, data layers, and forms. A server page can call the\ncontract-derived SDK, hydrate TanStack Query state, and hand interactive work to islands; routes\nthat need no island ship no island JavaScript.\n\nUse [the web-layer overview](/web-layer/) for the complete model, then follow the focused guides for\n[server rendering](/web-layer/server/), [page builders](/web-layer/builders/), and\n[queries and islands](/web-layer/query/).\n\n## 5. Observability connects the resource graph\n\nOne runtime, many resources. The generated AppHost composes apps, services, plugin processors,\ndatabases, and cache resources, then injects service-discovery and OTLP configuration into their\nprocesses. The Aspire dashboard reads health, logs, traces, and metrics from that running graph;\ntrace context carried by SDK calls keeps client and service spans connected.\n\nAspire is the default local orchestration layer, not part of every package API. Without it, the Deno\npackages remain usable, but resource startup, discovery values, and telemetry wiring become the\napplication's responsibility. Read [Orchestration & runtime](/orchestration-runtime/),\n[Observability](/observability/), or [the Aspire boundary](/explanation/aspire/) for mechanics.\n","numLines":73,"startLine":1,"totalLines":73}}}
