sequence; the dashboard reports *as* aspire starts. Minor.\n- \"carries logs and traces across it\" — \"across it\" is vague. \"across the resource graph\" would be clearer.\n\nLine 41-44 (lede paragraph):\n\"The definition correlates both events by `orderId`. When `inventory.failed` arrives, its handler returns a compensation request for `payment.captured`. Executing that request needs a saga bus bridge with a compensator; surviving a restart needs a durable store. With that wiring, the registered handler emits `payment.refund`.\"\n- \"a compensator\" — drift vs project terminology which uses \"compensation handler\". The `compensator` term appears in code (native.compensator) but in prose the project uses \"compensation handler\". Could confuse senior readers.\n- \"surviving a restart needs a durable store\" — passive construction; \"the saga needs a durable store to survive a restart\" is clearer.\n\nLine 49-52 nav: \"Run it / Understand it / Decide whether it fits\" — fine\n\n### why.vto\n\nLine 8-9 (locked tagline): \"For teams whose TypeScript app has become a system.\"\n- subhead: \"NetScript fits long-lived products where request handling, background work, browser UI, and operations have become one application; it trades pick-any-library freedom for a coordinated Deno workspace.\"\n- \"it trades pick-any-library freedom for a coordinated Deno workspace.\" — verb-driven and clear. Good.\n\nLine 16-18: \"If the product is a static site, a short-lived prototype, or one request/response process, start with Fresh or Hono. NetScript begins to pay for itself when the boundaries between processes, background work, and the browser have become part of the product.\"\n- \"begins to pay for itself\" — common idiom but borderline marketing-speak. Slight.\n\nLine 22-23: \"You shouldn't have to assemble a backend from a dozen libraries that have never met. The cost is not choosing them once; it is maintaining the seams between them:\"\n- \"have never met\" — personification, fine, slightly stylistic\n- \"The cost is not choosing them once; it is maintaining the seams between them:\" — fine, this is the \"not X but Y\" scaffolding the user asked me to flag. Wait — actually the rule says flag \"not X but Y\" scaffolding but this is a real claim that distinguishes one-time cost from maintenance cost. It's defensible. Hmm, but the prompt explicitly lists \"not X but Y\" as a slop marker. Let me read carefully: \"is not choosing them once; it is maintaining the seams between them\" — the use of negation + is/inversion pattern. Could be flagged as slop pattern. But this instance does have substantive meaning.\n\nActually re-reading the user's prompt: \"not X but Y\" scaffolding is the slop marker. The text \"The cost is not choosing them once; it is maintaining the seams between them:\" is a legitimate distinction. It distinguishes one-time cost from ongoing. Not slop.\n\nLine 33: \"That standing maintenance is the **integration tax**: unrelated tools pretending to be one system.\"\n- \"pretending to be one system\" — personification, fine\n\nLine 37-41: \"We didn't invent new primitives. NetScript makes two early architecture choices and carries them through the workspace: callers derive their types from shared contracts, and multi-step work runs through explicit durable state. Logging can be added later. Replacing parallel client definitions or ad-hoc effects after they spread through an application usually means changing every caller and every workflow.\"\n- Fine\n\nLine 43-45: \"The rest of NetScript makes those two decisions operational. Services serve the contract at request time, plugin runtimes own the work that outlives a request, and Aspire brings up the resources with their telemetry connected.\"\n- \"Aspire brings up the resources with their telemetry connected.\" — the \"their\" is a bit awkward (refers back to \"resources\"). Mild.\n\nLine 49: \"No row is a universal winner. Choose the system whose primary constraint matches yours.\"\n- Fine. Good.\n\nLine 55-58: \"NetScript uses Fresh and Hono as foundations, then adds service contracts, background runtimes, and an Aspire workspace. The cost is more framework and more project shape.\"\n- \"background runtimes\" vs \"plugin runtimes\" elsewhere — drift! In concepts.vto, why.vto L44, the term is \"plugin runtimes\". L57 uses \"background runtimes\" which describes what plugin runtimes *do* but doesn't match the project term. Drift.\n\nLine 62: \"React and the Next ecosystem are the center\" — \"the Next ecosystem\" is fine but the row title says \"Next.js\" while the type is \"the Next ecosystem\". OK.\n\nLine 67: \"Integrated infrastructure workflow is the center\" — OK\nLine 72: \"NetScript's saga core keeps TypeScript definitions, store and bus ports, transports, and compensation inside a broader application workspace. It is not a drop-in Temporal API.\"\n- \"store and bus ports\" — fine\n- \"It is not a drop-in Temporal API.\" — fine, declarative.\n\nLine 84-92: trade-offs section\n- \"APIs move\" — fine\n- \"If you need a frozen surface today, wait or budget for upgrades.\" — fine\n- \"Scaffold with `--no-aspire` to opt out\" — fine\n- \"resource wiring and local telemetry then belong to you\" — fine\n\n### quickstart.vto\n\nLine 7: `# Run a NetScript workspace, then change it` — heading style. Note other pages use H2 (`##`). This is the only H1 in the four pages.\n- Heading style parity: index, why, concepts all use no top-level heading after hero (page title in front-matter). quickstart has H1 in body. Slight drift in heading style.\n\nLine 9-10: \"This path installs the CLI, scaffolds one example service, starts the generated resource graph, and makes one visible edit.\"\n- \"and makes one visible edit\" — hmm, a sequence that pivots at the end. \"make one visible edit\" is a different kind of step. Not slop.\n\nLine 12-16: Prerequisites callout\n- \"Install Deno 2.x, the .NET Aspire CLI, and start Docker before continuing. The default Postgres and cache resources run in containers.\"\n- \"and start Docker before continuing\" — slightly awkward phrasing (start Docker? Docker is a daemon, \"start Docker Desktop\" or \"have Docker running\" would be more conventional). Minor copy issue. Senior readers may parse it as ambiguous.\n\nLine 27: `netscript init my-app --db postgres --service --yes`\nLine 30-32: \"The `--service` flag is explicit: without it, `netscript init` does not scaffold the example oRPC service. `--yes` accepts the remaining defaults without prompts, including the `dashboard` app name and shared Redis cache used below.\"\n- Fine.\n\nLine 36: \"**Nothing runs until this step.**\" — emphatic short statement. Fine.\n\nLine 44-46: \"Use the dashboard URL and one-time login token printed by `aspire start`. Wait until the Fresh app, example service, Postgres, and shared cache report healthy, then open the Fresh app from its resource link. The page heading should read `my-app`.\"\n- \"one-time login token\" — minor: Aspire uses \"login token\" or \"API token\" — but the precise term in current Aspire versions is a token displayed once. Fine.\n\nLine 49-51: \"The AppHost must start Postgres before a migration, generation, or seed command can connect to it.\"\n- Fine, accurate.\n\nLine 56-59: \"Open `apps/dashboard/routes/(_components)/home-view.tsx`. Find the wrapped introductory sentence that starts with `A generated NetScript workspace` and replace that sentence with `This checkout dashboard is ours.` Save the file, then refresh the Fresh app URL from Aspire.\"\n- \"Find the wrapped introductory sentence that starts with `A generated NetScript workspace` and replace that sentence\" — slightly verbose. Could be \"Replace the introductory sentence starting with `A generated NetScript workspace`.\" Minor.\n\nLine 64-67: Stop command\n- Fine.\n\n### concepts.vto\n\nHero: tagline + subhead. Note em-dash use: \"Contracts define the boundaries. Everything else — services, plugin runtimes,