scovery\" (two words). \nLine 65: \"inject service-discovery and OTLP configuration\" — \"service-discovery\" (hyphenated). \nSame concept, two styles in the same file. Drift.\n\nLine 42-45: \"The host is empty; plugins fill it. A plugin manifest is validated data that declares services, background processors, schemas, topics, configuration, and telemetry contributions. The CLI turns those declarations into static workspace wiring, and Aspire can materialize a plugin's API and background processors as separate resources.\"\n- \"background processors\" — consistent with quickstart?\n- \"schemas, topics, configuration, and telemetry contributions\" — wide enumeration but factual.\n\nLine 47-49: \"\"One plugin\" is a packaging unit, not a runtime unit. Workers, sagas, triggers, and streams are the durable examples; the full manifest and registry mechanics live in [the plugin-system explanation](/explanation/plugin-system/).\"\n- \"\"One plugin\" is a packaging unit, not a runtime unit.\" — could be flagged as \"not X but Y\" scaffolding but the meaning is precise: this is a real distinction the project makes. Skip.\n\nLine 53-56: \"`@netscript/fresh` builds on Fresh 2 and Preact. Typed route contracts and `definePage()` connect paths, search parameters, metadata, handlers, data layers, and forms. A server page can call the contract-derived SDK, hydrate TanStack Query state, and hand interactive work to islands; routes that need no island ship no island JavaScript.\"\n- Fine, accurate.\n\nLine 58-60: \"Use [the web-layer overview](/web-layer/) for the complete model, then follow the focused guides for [server rendering](/web-layer/server/), [page builders](/web-layer/builders/), and [queries and islands](/web-layer/query/).\"\n- Fine.\n\nLine 62: \"## 5. Observability connects the resource graph\"\n- Fine.\n\nLine 64-67: \"One runtime, many resources. The generated AppHost composes apps, services, plugin processors, databases, and cache resources, then injects service-discovery and OTLP configuration into their processes. The Aspire dashboard reads health, logs, traces, and metrics from that running graph; trace context carried by SDK calls keeps client and service spans connected.\"\n- \"service-discovery\" hyphenated again. Same drift.\n- \"the Aspire dashboard reads health, logs, traces, and metrics from that running graph\" — accurate.\n\nLine 69-72: \"Aspire is the default local orchestration layer, not part of every package API. Without it, the Deno packages remain usable, but resource startup, discovery values, and telemetry wiring become the application's responsibility.\"\n- \"Aspire is the default local orchestration layer, not part of every package API.\" — \"not X\" pattern. Could be flagged. But the meaning is precise. Skip.\n\nOK now I have my list. Let me also check headings style parity more carefully:\n\n- index.vto: no top-level heading after hero — relies on hero tagline\n- why.vto: no top-level heading after hero — relies on hero tagline\n- quickstart.vto: H1 \"Run a NetScript workspace, then change it\" — body H1\n- concepts.vto: no top-level heading after hero — relies on hero tagline, then numbered H2 sections\n\nSo quickstart diverges: it has a body H1 while the others rely on hero. Either all should have hero-only, or all should have a body H1. This is a heading-style parity issue.\n\nAlso section heading style in quickstart uses \"## 1. Install the CLI\" with numbering, while concepts also uses \"## 1. Contracts define the boundary\" with numbering. Why.vto uses \"## The integration tax\" without numbering. So numbered vs unnumbered section headings is consistent between quickstart and concepts but not why. why.vto's sections are \"The integration tax\" / \"The two decisions that are expensive to retrofit\" / \"Compare the center of gravity\" / \"The trade-offs are real\" — none numbered.\n\nThis is a section-heading style drift: numbered sections vs unnumbered.\n\nLet me also note subhead style:\n- index